    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Gem25P Vorthos is resolving their own Pithing Needle's enter-the-battlefield trigger and must name a card. They named Urza's Saga, which is their OWN card currently on their battlefield. Pithing Needle shuts down activated abilities of the named card, so by naming Urza's Saga they are locking out their own Saga's activated abilities (the {T}: Add {C} and the {2}, {T}: Create Construct token abilities). The opponent's board has only Island and Field of Ruin — Field of Ruin has an activated ability that could be problematic (it can destroy nonbasic lands like Urza's Saga), but it requires the opponent to pay mana they may not have. Meanwhile, the opponent has Hedron Crabs in the graveyard and a mill strategy. Naming Shadowspear would also be bad since it's their own card. Given the limited choices of Shadowspear and Urza's Saga, the player should have named Shadowspear (less impactful to lock out) since Urza's Saga is actively providing value through its chapter abilities and construct token creation. However, looking more carefully at the choices presented — they could only choose between Shadowspear and Urza's Saga — both are their own permanents being offered as naming options. This appears to be an interface limitation. Naming Urza's Saga locks out their Saga's mana and construct abilities, which is a significant self-inflicted wound. Naming Shadowspear would only lock out the equip ability and the hexproof/indestructible removal ability, which is much less costly since the Shadowspear is already equipped or can remain as a static buff.",
      "action_taken": "Named Urza's Saga for Pithing Needle, shutting down their own Urza's Saga activated abilities (colorless mana production and Construct token creation).",
      "better_line": "Name Shadowspear for Pithing Needle instead, as losing Shadowspear's activated abilities (equip and the hexproof/indestructible removal) is far less costly than losing Urza's Saga's mana and token generation. Ideally, they should have named an opponent's card like Field of Ruin if the interface allowed it.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 94,
      "player": "Gem25P Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 66,
    },
